==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|tanþs|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|h₃dónts||tooth|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognates with Old Frisian {{term|toth|tōth}}, Old Saxon {{term|tand}}, Dutch {{term|tand}}, Old High German {{term|zan}}, {{term|zand}} (German {{term|Zahn}}), Old Norse {{term|tǫnn}} (Swedish {{term|tand}}), Gothic {{term|𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌸𐌿𐍃|sc=Goth}}; and with Sanskrit {{term|दन्त|sc=Deva}} (Hindi {{term|दांत़|sc=Deva}}), Ancient Greek {{term|ὀδών|lang=grc}}, Latin {{term|dens}} (French {{term|dent|lang=fr}}), Old Irish {{term|dét}}, Lithuanian {{term|dantis|dantìs}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/toːθ/|lang=ang}}

